// Expanded public-exam notes: Producer Behaviour (Module VII).
window.COURSE_LESSONS.find(x=>x.n===17).content = `
<h3>Lesson 17 — Production Function</h3><p><b>Public examination · Producer Behaviour.</b> This module carries 22 marks in the syllabus weightage table used in this app. That is a module total, not a promise of 22 marks from this lesson.</p>
<h3>1. Meaning</h3><p>A <b>production function</b> shows the technical relationship between inputs (factors of production) and the maximum output that can be produced with those inputs, with technology held constant.</p><div class="formula">Q = f(L, K, Land, Organisation)</div><p>In the short run, at least one factor is fixed. For example, a factory may have a fixed building and machines but can change the number of workers.</p>
<h3>2. Total, Average and Marginal Product</h3><ul><li><b>Total Product (TP):</b> total output produced by all units of the variable factor.</li><li><b>Average Product (AP):</b> output per unit of the variable factor.</li><li><b>Marginal Product (MP):</b> extra output from employing one additional unit of the variable factor.</li></ul><div class="formula">AP = TP ÷ units of variable factor<br>MP = Change in TP ÷ Change in variable factor</div>
<div class="worked"><b>Worked example</b><p>Workers: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. Total output: 10, 24, 36, 44, 48.</p><table class="study-table"><tr><th>Workers</th><th>TP</th><th>MP</th><th>AP</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>10</td><td>10</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>24</td><td>14</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>36</td><td>12</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>44</td><td>8</td><td>11</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>48</td><td>4</td><td>9.6</td></tr></table><p>At worker 2, MP = 24 − 10 = 14. AP at worker 4 = 44 ÷ 4 = 11.</p></div>
<h3>3. Law of Variable Proportions</h3><p>When more units of a variable factor are combined with fixed factors, output first increases at an increasing rate, then at a decreasing rate, and may eventually fall. This is the law of variable proportions.</p><ol><li><b>Stage I — Increasing returns:</b> TP rises at an increasing rate; MP generally rises. Better use of fixed factors and specialisation can explain this stage.</li><li><b>Stage II — Diminishing returns:</b> TP continues to rise but at a decreasing rate; MP is positive but falls. A rational producer normally operates in this stage.</li><li><b>Stage III — Negative returns:</b> TP falls and MP becomes negative because too many variable-factor units crowd the fixed factor.</li></ol>
<div class="diagram"><b>Typical TP curve (illustrative)</b><svg viewBox="0 0 330 180" role="img" aria-label="Total product rises quickly, then more slowly, reaches a maximum and falls"><path d="M35 12V148H310" fill="none" stroke="#172334" stroke-width="1.5"/><path d="M40 142 C75 125 95 70 145 48 S220 26 250 35 S280 68 300 92" fill="none" stroke="#245a91" stroke-width="3"/><path d="M40 142L40 142" stroke="#245a91"/><path d="M145 48V148M250 35V148" stroke="#b87916" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="80" y="160" font-size="9" fill="#172334">Stage I</text><text x="190" y="160" font-size="9" fill="#172334">Stage II</text><text x="270" y="160" font-size="9" fill="#172334">III</text><text x="12" y="18" font-size="9" fill="#172334">TP</text><text x="300" y="174" font-size="9" fill="#172334">Variable input</text></svg><small>Conceptual shape only; not drawn to scale.</small></div>
<p><b>Important relationships:</b> when MP is greater than AP, AP rises; when MP is less than AP, AP falls; MP intersects AP at AP's maximum. When MP is zero, TP is at its maximum (before it begins to fall).</p>
<h3>Exam practice</h3><ol><li>Define production function.</li><li>Differentiate TP, AP and MP.</li><li>Calculate AP and MP from a small table.</li><li>Explain the three stages of the law of variable proportions.</li><li>Why does MP eventually fall when one factor is fixed?</li></ol>
<p class="note">Remember: MP is the change in TP, not TP divided by labour. AP is TP divided by labour.</p>`;

window.COURSE_LESSONS.find(x=>x.n===18).content = `
<h3>Lesson 18 — Cost of Production</h3><p><b>Public examination · Producer Behaviour.</b></p>
<h3>1. Meaning and types of cost</h3><p>Cost of production is the expenditure or sacrifice incurred by a producer to produce goods and services.</p><ul><li><b>Fixed cost (TFC):</b> does not change with output in the short run, e.g. factory rent.</li><li><b>Variable cost (TVC):</b> changes with output, e.g. raw materials and hourly labour.</li><li><b>Total cost (TC):</b> total fixed cost plus total variable cost.</li><li><b>Explicit cost:</b> actual money payment, e.g. wages paid.</li><li><b>Implicit cost:</b> estimated value of self-owned resources used, e.g. owner's own building.</li></ul><div class="formula">TC = TFC + TVC</div>
<h3>2. Average and marginal costs</h3><div class="formula">AFC = TFC ÷ Q<br>AVC = TVC ÷ Q<br>AC (ATC) = TC ÷ Q = AFC + AVC<br>MC = Change in TC ÷ Change in Q</div><p>Q means units of output. Marginal cost is the extra cost of producing one more unit.</p>
<div class="worked"><b>Worked example</b><p>At output 5, TFC = ₹100 and TVC = ₹150.</p><ul><li>TC = 100 + 150 = ₹250</li><li>AFC = 100 ÷ 5 = ₹20</li><li>AVC = 150 ÷ 5 = ₹30</li><li>AC = 250 ÷ 5 = ₹50</li></ul><p>If TC rises from ₹250 to ₹286 when output rises from 5 to 6, MC of the 6th unit = 286 − 250 = ₹36.</p></div>
<h3>3. Important cost-curve relationships</h3><ul><li>TFC is horizontal because it remains constant in the short run.</li><li>TVC rises as output increases.</li><li>TC starts at the level of TFC and rises with TVC.</li><li>AFC continuously falls as output increases.</li><li>AVC and AC are usually U-shaped in the standard short-run model.</li><li>MC cuts AVC and AC at their minimum points.</li></ul>
<div class="diagram"><b>Typical short-run average and marginal cost curves</b><svg viewBox="0 0 330 180" role="img" aria-label="U-shaped AFC, AVC, AC and MC curves, with MC crossing AVC and AC at their minimums"><path d="M30 10V148H315" fill="none" stroke="#172334" stroke-width="1.5"/><path d="M35 30 C80 65 115 110 170 115 S250 90 300 70" fill="none" stroke="#16845b" stroke-width="2.5"/><path d="M35 90 C95 120 130 125 180 95 S245 60 300 48" fill="none" stroke="#245a91" stroke-width="2.5"/><path d="M35 60 C90 95 135 105 185 78 S250 40 300 28" fill="none" stroke="#b87916" stroke-width="2.5"/><path d="M35 142 C100 138 140 118 170 78 S220 25 300 12" fill="none" stroke="#a34c76" stroke-width="2.5"/><text x="300" y="68" font-size="10" fill="#16845b">AFC</text><text x="300" y="48" font-size="10" fill="#245a91">AVC</text><text x="300" y="27" font-size="10" fill="#b87916">AC</text><text x="300" y="12" font-size="10" fill="#a34c76">MC</text><text x="7" y="16" font-size="9" fill="#172334">Cost</text><text x="285" y="166" font-size="9" fill="#172334">Output</text></svg><small>Schematic only. Focus on U-shape and the MC/minimum relationships.</small></div>
<h3>Exam practice</h3><ol><li>Differentiate fixed and variable costs.</li><li>Write the relationship between TC, TFC and TVC.</li><li>Calculate AFC, AVC and AC from given data.</li><li>Define marginal cost and calculate it from a change in total cost.</li><li>Why does AFC fall as output rises?</li><li>Explain why MC cuts AC at AC's minimum point.</li></ol>`;

window.COURSE_LESSONS.find(x=>x.n===19).content = `
<h3>Lesson 19 — Supply</h3><p><b>Public examination · Producer Behaviour.</b></p>
<h3>1. Meaning of supply</h3><p>Supply is the quantity of a good that a seller is willing and able to sell at different prices during a given period, other things remaining constant.</p><p><b>Law of supply:</b> other things remaining the same, quantity supplied generally rises when price rises and falls when price falls. Price and quantity supplied have a direct relationship.</p>
<div class="diagram"><b>Typical upward-sloping supply curve</b><svg viewBox="0 0 320 180" role="img" aria-label="Supply curve slopes upward from left to right"><path d="M35 12V148H300" fill="none" stroke="#172334" stroke-width="1.5"/><path d="M55 135L270 30" stroke="#16845b" stroke-width="3" fill="none"/><text x="274" y="27" font-size="12" fill="#16845b">S</text><text x="8" y="18" font-size="10" fill="#172334">Price</text><text x="245" y="168" font-size="10" fill="#172334">Quantity</text></svg></div>
<h3>2. Individual and market supply</h3><p><b>Individual supply</b> is the quantity offered by one seller. <b>Market supply</b> is the total quantity offered by all sellers at each price, found by adding individual quantities supplied horizontally.</p>
<h3>3. Determinants of supply</h3><ul><li>Price of the good.</li><li>Prices of inputs such as wages and raw materials.</li><li>Technology and productivity.</li><li>Taxes and subsidies.</li><li>Number of sellers.</li><li>Expectations about future prices.</li><li>Natural conditions, especially for agricultural goods.</li></ul>
<h3>4. Movement versus shift</h3><p><b>Change in quantity supplied:</b> caused by a change in the good's own price; movement along the same supply curve.</p><p><b>Change in supply:</b> caused by a non-price factor such as technology, input prices or taxes; the entire curve shifts.</p>
<div class="diagram"><b>Movement along versus shift of supply</b><svg viewBox="0 0 330 180" role="img" aria-label="Two upward supply curves S1 and S2 shifted right; arrows on S1 show movement along the same curve"><path d="M30 10V145H310" fill="none" stroke="#172334" stroke-width="1.5"/><path d="M55 132L190 30" stroke="#245a91" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none"/><path d="M105 132L240 30" stroke="#16845b" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none"/><text x="194" y="27" font-size="10" fill="#245a91">S1</text><text x="245" y="27" font-size="10" fill="#16845b">S2</text><path d="M100 98L135 72" stroke="#b87916" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M130 72L135 72L133 78" fill="none" stroke="#b87916" stroke-width="2"/><text x="145" y="88" font-size="9" fill="#b87916">Movement on S1</text><text x="165" y="165" font-size="9" fill="#16845b">Right shift = increase in supply</text></svg></div>
<h3>Exam practice</h3><ol><li>State the law of supply.</li><li>Differentiate individual and market supply.</li><li>Explain four determinants of supply.</li><li>Differentiate change in quantity supplied and change in supply.</li><li>Draw and label an upward-sloping supply curve.</li></ol>
<div class="callout"><b>Memory tip:</b> Own price changes = movement. Other factors change = shift.</div>`;

window.COURSE_LESSONS.find(x=>x.n===20).content = `
<h3>Lesson 20 — Price Elasticity of Supply</h3><p><b>Public examination · Producer Behaviour.</b></p>
<h3>1. Meaning</h3><p>Price elasticity of supply measures how much quantity supplied responds to a change in price, other things remaining constant.</p><div class="formula">Es = (% change in quantity supplied) ÷ (% change in price)</div><p>For a straight-line calculation from initial values:</p><div class="formula">Es = (Change in Q ÷ Original Q) × 100 ÷ [(Change in P ÷ Original P) × 100]</div><p>The percentage signs cancel, so Es = (ΔQ / Q) ÷ (ΔP / P).</p>
<div class="worked"><b>Worked example</b><p>Price rises from ₹10 to ₹12 (20% increase). Quantity supplied rises from 100 to 130 units (30% increase).</p><div class="formula">Es = 30% ÷ 20% = 1.5</div><p>Supply is elastic because Es is greater than 1.</p></div>
<h3>2. Degrees of elasticity</h3><ul><li><b>Perfectly inelastic:</b> Es = 0; quantity supplied does not respond.</li><li><b>Relatively inelastic:</b> Es is less than 1.</li><li><b>Unitary elastic:</b> Es = 1.</li><li><b>Relatively elastic:</b> Es is greater than 1.</li><li><b>Perfectly elastic:</b> Es is extremely large; sellers supply any quantity at one price in the idealised model.</li></ul>
<div class="diagram"><b>Supply elasticity — visual idea</b><svg viewBox="0 0 330 165" role="img" aria-label="A steep supply curve indicates relatively inelastic supply and a flatter curve indicates relatively elastic supply"><path d="M30 12V135H310" fill="none" stroke="#172334" stroke-width="1.5"/><path d="M55 130L100 25" stroke="#b87916" stroke-width="3"/><path d="M55 130L270 75" stroke="#245a91" stroke-width="3"/><text x="104" y="24" font-size="9" fill="#b87916">Steep: less elastic</text><text x="215" y="65" font-size="9" fill="#245a91">Flat: more elastic</text><text x="8" y="17" font-size="9" fill="#172334">Price</text><text x="260" y="153" font-size="9" fill="#172334">Quantity</text></svg><small>For a given price change, a flatter supply curve represents a larger quantity response.</small></div>
<h3>3. Factors affecting elasticity of supply</h3><ul><li><b>Time period:</b> supply is usually more elastic when producers have more time to adjust.</li><li><b>Spare capacity:</b> unused machines and workers make it easier to raise output.</li><li><b>Availability of inputs:</b> easily available raw materials and labour make supply more elastic.</li><li><b>Storage:</b> storable goods can often be supplied more flexibly.</li><li><b>Nature of production:</b> crops may be difficult to increase immediately after planting.</li></ul>
<h3>Exam practice</h3><ol><li>Define price elasticity of supply and write its formula.</li><li>If price rises by 10% and quantity supplied rises by 25%, calculate Es.</li><li>Explain elastic, inelastic and unitary elastic supply.</li><li>Explain four factors affecting elasticity of supply.</li><li>Draw a steep and a flat supply curve and label them.</li></ol>
<p class="note">Common mistake: do not divide percentage change in price by percentage change in quantity. Quantity percentage change goes on top.</p>`;


// Expanded public-exam notes: Statistical Tools (Module IV).
window.COURSE_LESSONS.find(x=>x.n===8).content = `
<h3>Lesson 8 — Measures of Central Tendency</h3><p><b>Public examination · Statistical Tools.</b></p>
<h3>1. Meaning</h3><p>A measure of central tendency gives one central or representative value for a data set. The main measures are arithmetic mean, median and mode. Quartiles divide ordered data into four parts.</p>
<h3>2. Arithmetic mean</h3><div class="formula">Individual series: Mean = ΣX ÷ N<br>Discrete/continuous series: Mean = ΣfX ÷ Σf<br>Weighted mean = ΣwX ÷ Σw<br>Combined mean = (n₁x̄₁ + n₂x̄₂) ÷ (n₁+n₂)</div><p>For a continuous series, use the class midpoint as X. Frequency is f; N is total frequency.</p><div class="worked"><b>Example</b><p>Values 4, 6, 8, 10: mean = (4+6+8+10) ÷ 4 = 28 ÷ 4 = 7.</p><p>For values 2 and 5 with frequencies 3 and 2: mean = (2×3 + 5×2) ÷ 5 = 16 ÷ 5 = 3.2.</p></div>
<h3>3. Median</h3><p>Median is the middle value after arranging observations in ascending or descending order.</p><ul><li>Odd N: median is the value at position (N+1)/2.</li><li>Even N: median is the average of values at positions N/2 and (N/2)+1.</li></ul><div class="formula">Grouped continuous series: Median = l + [(N/2 − cf) ÷ f] × h</div><p>l = lower boundary of median class; cf = cumulative frequency before it; f = frequency of median class; h = class width.</p>
<h3>4. Mode and quartiles</h3><p><b>Mode</b> is the value occurring most frequently. For a grouped continuous series:</p><div class="formula">Mode = l + [(f₁ − f₀) ÷ (2f₁ − f₀ − f₂)] × h</div><p>f₁ = modal-class frequency; f₀ = preceding frequency; f₂ = succeeding frequency.</p><p><b>Quartiles:</b> Q1 is the value below which 25% of observations lie; Q2 is the median; Q3 is the value below which 75% lie. For grouped data, use the median-style formula with N/4 for Q1 and 3N/4 for Q3.</p>
<h3>5. Which average should you use?</h3><table class="study-table"><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Useful when</th><th>Limitation</th></tr><tr><td>Mean</td><td>All values matter and data are numerical.</td><td>Affected by extreme values.</td></tr><tr><td>Median</td><td>Data are skewed or contain extreme values.</td><td>Does not use every value's exact size.</td></tr><tr><td>Mode</td><td>Most common value/category is needed.</td><td>May have more than one mode or none.</td></tr></table>
<h3>Exam practice</h3><ol><li>Calculate mean of 5, 7, 8, 10.</li><li>Find the median of 3, 9, 5, 7, 11.</li><li>Calculate mean for a frequency table using ΣfX/Σf.</li><li>Write the grouped median and mode formulas and explain each symbol.</li><li>Give one advantage and one limitation of mean, median and mode.</li></ol>
<p class="note">Always arrange individual data before finding the median. In grouped questions, identify the correct median/modal class first.</p>`;

window.COURSE_LESSONS.find(x=>x.n===9).content = `
<h3>Lesson 9 — Measures of Dispersion</h3><p><b>Public examination · Statistical Tools.</b></p>
<h3>1. Meaning</h3><p>Dispersion measures how far observations are spread around a central value. Two groups can have the same mean but very different spread.</p>
<h3>2. Main measures and formulas</h3><div class="formula">Range = Largest value − Smallest value<br>Coefficient of Range = (L − S) ÷ (L + S)<br>Quartile Deviation (QD) = (Q3 − Q1) ÷ 2<br>Coefficient of QD = (Q3 − Q1) ÷ (Q3 + Q1)<br>Mean Deviation = Σ|X − A| ÷ N<br>Grouped MD = Σf|X − A| ÷ Σf</div><p>L = largest value; S = smallest value; A = mean or median as specified. Absolute signs mean ignore the plus/minus sign.</p>
<h3>3. Standard deviation</h3><p>Standard deviation measures the typical spread around the arithmetic mean. A smaller SD means values are more closely clustered around the mean.</p><div class="formula">SD (individual) = √[Σ(X − X̄)² ÷ N]<br>SD (frequency) = √[Σf(X − X̄)² ÷ Σf]<br>Coefficient of Variation = (SD ÷ Mean) × 100</div><div class="worked"><b>Simple example</b><p>For 2, 4, 6: mean = 4. Squared deviations are 4, 0, 4. Variance = 8/3. SD = √(8/3) ≈ 1.63.</p></div>
<h3>4. Comparing consistency</h3><p>Use the coefficient of variation (CV) to compare variability between series with different means or units. The lower CV generally indicates greater consistency (less relative variation).</p>
<h3>5. Lorenz curve</h3><p>A Lorenz curve is a graphical way to show inequality in the distribution of income or wealth. The 45° line represents perfect equality. The farther the Lorenz curve lies below that line, the greater the inequality shown.</p>
<div class="diagram"><b>Lorenz curve — conceptual</b><svg viewBox="0 0 320 180" role="img" aria-label="Equality line and Lorenz curve below it"><path d="M35 12V145H300" fill="none" stroke="#172334" stroke-width="1.5"/><path d="M35 145L280 20" stroke="#245a91" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><path d="M35 145 C100 140 150 125 190 95 S250 45 280 20" stroke="#16845b" stroke-width="3" fill="none"/><text x="180" y="25" font-size="9" fill="#245a91">Perfect equality</text><text x="130" y="115" font-size="9" fill="#16845b">Lorenz curve</text><text x="8" y="17" font-size="9" fill="#172334">Income share</text><text x="160" y="165" font-size="9" fill="#172334">Population share</text></svg></div>
<h3>Exam practice</h3><ol><li>Define dispersion and explain its need.</li><li>Calculate range and coefficient of range.</li><li>Calculate QD from Q1 and Q3.</li><li>Write the standard deviation and coefficient of variation formulas.</li><li>What does a lower CV indicate?</li><li>Explain how a Lorenz curve shows inequality.</li></ol>`;

window.COURSE_LESSONS.find(x=>x.n===10).content = `
<h3>Lesson 10 — Correlation Analysis</h3><p><b>Public examination · Statistical Tools.</b></p>
<h3>1. Meaning and types</h3><p>Correlation measures the direction and degree of association between two variables. Positive correlation means they tend to move in the same direction; negative correlation means they tend to move in opposite directions; zero correlation means no linear association is indicated.</p>
<ul><li><b>Perfect positive:</b> r = +1.</li><li><b>Perfect negative:</b> r = −1.</li><li><b>No linear correlation:</b> r = 0.</li></ul>
<h3>2. Scatter diagram</h3><p>Plot each paired observation (X,Y) as a point. An upward pattern suggests positive correlation; a downward pattern suggests negative correlation; a random cloud suggests weak or no linear correlation.</p>
<div class="diagram"><b>Scatter diagram patterns</b><svg viewBox="0 0 330 160" role="img" aria-label="Positive, negative and weak scatter patterns"><g stroke="#172334" fill="none"><path d="M12 12V130H105M120 12V130H213M228 12V130H320"/></g><g fill="#245a91"><circle cx="25" cy="112" r="3"/><circle cx="39" cy="96" r="3"/><circle cx="53" cy="88" r="3"/><circle cx="68" cy="62" r="3"/><circle cx="83" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="96" cy="28" r="3"/><circle cx="133" cy="30" r="3"/><circle cx="149" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="165" cy="65" r="3"/><circle cx="180" cy="88" r="3"/><circle cx="197" cy="112" r="3"/><circle cx="238" cy="70" r="3"/><circle cx="251" cy="28" r="3"/><circle cx="264" cy="105" r="3"/><circle cx="277" cy="58" r="3"/><circle cx="291" cy="91" r="3"/><circle cx="307" cy="42" r="3"/></g><text x="55" y="148" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#16845b">Positive</text><text x="166" y="148" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#16845b">Negative</text><text x="274" y="148" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#16845b">Weak</text></svg></div>
<h3>3. Karl Pearson's coefficient</h3><div class="formula">r = [nΣXY − ΣXΣY] ÷ √{[nΣX² − (ΣX)²][nΣY² − (ΣY)²]}</div><p>The value of r always lies between −1 and +1. The sign gives direction; the absolute size indicates strength of linear association.</p>
<h3>4. Spearman's rank correlation</h3><p>Use ranks when data are given as ranks or can be ranked.</p><div class="formula">ρ = 1 − [6Σd² ÷ n(n² − 1)]</div><p>d = difference between paired ranks; n = number of pairs. This simple formula assumes no tied ranks; ties require a correction.</p>
<div class="callout"><b>Important:</b> Correlation does not prove causation. Two variables may move together because of another factor or coincidence.</div>
<h3>Exam practice</h3><ol><li>Define correlation and state its types.</li><li>Draw positive, negative and zero/weak scatter patterns.</li><li>What is the range of Karl Pearson's r?</li><li>When is Spearman rank correlation useful?</li><li>Explain why correlation does not prove cause and effect.</li></ol>`;

window.COURSE_LESSONS.find(x=>x.n===11).content = `
<h3>Lesson 11 — Index Numbers</h3><p><b>Public examination · Statistical Tools.</b></p>
<h3>1. Meaning and uses</h3><p>An index number is a statistical measure that shows the relative change in one or more variables over time or across places, usually with a base period set to 100.</p><p>Uses include measuring price changes, cost of living, production trends and changes in purchasing power. Index numbers help governments, businesses and workers make decisions.</p>
<h3>2. Simple index numbers</h3><div class="formula">Simple price relative = (Current price ÷ Base price) × 100<br>Simple aggregative price index = (Σp₁ ÷ Σp₀) × 100</div><p>p₀ = base-period price; p₁ = current-period price. If an index is 125, the measured level is 25% above the base-period level.</p>
<h3>3. Weighted price index numbers</h3><p>Weights give greater importance to items with larger quantities or expenditure shares.</p><div class="formula">Laspeyres = (Σp₁q₀ ÷ Σp₀q₀) × 100<br>Paasche = (Σp₁q₁ ÷ Σp₀q₁) × 100<br>Fisher's Ideal = √(Laspeyres × Paasche)</div><p>q₀ = base-period quantities; q₁ = current-period quantities. Laspeyres uses base quantities; Paasche uses current quantities; Fisher is the geometric mean of the two indices.</p>
<div class="worked"><b>Worked example: price relative</b><p>A product costs ₹40 in the base year and ₹50 now.</p><div class="formula">Index = (50 ÷ 40) × 100 = 125</div><p>Price is 25% higher than in the base year.</p></div>
<h3>4. Consumer Price Index (CPI)</h3><p>CPI measures changes over time in the prices of a selected basket of goods and services consumed by a defined group. It is used as an indicator of consumer inflation and cost-of-living changes. The basket and weights matter.</p>
<h3>5. Limitations</h3><ul><li>Results depend on the choice of base year and basket.</li><li>Consumer habits and product quality may change.</li><li>One index may not represent every household.</li><li>Data collection and weighting can introduce errors.</li></ul>
<h3>Exam practice</h3><ol><li>Define an index number and state four uses.</li><li>Calculate a simple price relative.</li><li>Differentiate Laspeyres and Paasche indices.</li><li>What is Fisher's Ideal Index?</li><li>Explain the meaning and limitations of CPI.</li></ol>
<p class="note">In numericals, label base-period values with 0 and current-period values with 1. Write the formula before substituting values.</p>`;


// Exam-ready teaching notes for the remaining public-examination lessons.
window.COURSE_LESSONS.find(x=>x.n===12).content = `
<h3>Lesson 12 — Introduction to the Study of Economics</h3><p><b>Public examination · Introduction to Economics.</b></p>
<h3>Meaning of economics</h3><p>Economics studies how people and society use scarce resources with alternative uses to satisfy wants. Because resources are limited, choices must be made and every choice has an opportunity cost.</p>
<h3>Microeconomics and macroeconomics</h3><table class="study-table"><tr><th>Microeconomics</th><th>Macroeconomics</th></tr><tr><td>Studies individual units such as a consumer, firm or one market.</td><td>Studies the economy as a whole.</td></tr><tr><td>Examples: price of tea, demand of one household, output of one firm.</td><td>Examples: national income, general price level, unemployment and total output.</td></tr></table>
<h3>Positive and normative economics</h3><ul><li><b>Positive:</b> statements about what is or what happens; can be tested with evidence. Example: “A rise in price reduces quantity demanded, other things equal.”</li><li><b>Normative:</b> statements about what ought to be; involve value judgements. Example: “The government should reduce inequality.”</li></ul>
<h3>Exam practice</h3><ol><li>Define economics using scarcity and choice.</li><li>Differentiate microeconomics and macroeconomics with two examples each.</li><li>Differentiate positive and normative statements.</li><li>Why does every economic choice involve an opportunity cost?</li></ol>`;

window.COURSE_LESSONS.find(x=>x.n===13).content = `
<h3>Lesson 13 — Central Problems of an Economy</h3><p><b>Public examination · Introduction to Economics.</b></p>
<h3>Why do economic problems arise?</h3><p>Human wants are many, but resources are limited and have alternative uses. Scarcity forces every economy to make choices.</p>
<h3>Three central problems</h3><ol><li><b>What to produce?</b> Which goods and services, and in what quantities?</li><li><b>How to produce?</b> Which production technique—labour-intensive or capital-intensive?</li><li><b>For whom to produce?</b> How will output and income be distributed among people?</li></ol>
<h3>Production Possibility Frontier (PPF)</h3><p>A PPF shows the maximum possible combinations of two goods that an economy can produce with given resources and technology, when resources are fully and efficiently used.</p>
<div class="diagram"><b>Production possibility frontier</b><svg viewBox="0 0 320 180" role="img" aria-label="A bowed-out PPF curve between good X and good Y"><path d="M35 12V145H300" fill="none" stroke="#172334" stroke-width="1.5"/><path d="M50 25 C75 70 150 120 280 135" fill="none" stroke="#245a91" stroke-width="3"/><text x="8" y="17" font-size="9" fill="#172334">Good Y</text><text x="265" y="164" font-size="9" fill="#172334">Good X</text><text x="63" y="18" font-size="9" fill="#245a91">PPF</text><text x="205" y="62" font-size="9" fill="#16845b">Inside = underuse</text></svg><small>Points on the frontier are efficient; inside points show underused resources; points outside are currently unattainable.</small></div>
<p><b>Opportunity cost</b> is the next best alternative sacrificed when a choice is made. <b>Marginal opportunity cost</b> is the extra quantity of one good sacrificed to produce one more unit of another good. A bowed-out PPF shows increasing marginal opportunity cost.</p>
<p>A PPF shifts outward when resources or technology improve; it shifts inward when productive capacity is destroyed.</p>
<h3>Exam practice</h3><ol><li>Explain the three central problems.</li><li>Define opportunity cost.</li><li>What does a point inside/on/outside a PPF mean?</li><li>Why is a PPF usually bowed outward?</li></ol>`;

window.COURSE_LESSONS.find(x=>x.n===14).content = `
<h3>Lesson 14 — Consumer's Equilibrium</h3><p><b>Public examination · Consumer Behaviour.</b></p>
<h3>1. Utility</h3><p>Utility is the want-satisfying power of a good. Total utility (TU) is total satisfaction from consumption; marginal utility (MU) is the change in TU from one extra unit.</p><div class="formula">MU = Change in TU ÷ Change in quantity</div><p><b>Law of diminishing marginal utility:</b> as a consumer consumes more units of a good, its marginal utility generally falls, other things remaining constant.</p>
<h3>2. Consumer equilibrium — utility approach</h3><p>With one good, a consumer reaches equilibrium when MU equals price in utility terms (when marginal utility of money is treated as constant). With two goods, the equi-marginal principle is:</p><div class="formula">MUx ÷ Px = MUy ÷ Py</div><p>The consumer allocates spending so that marginal utility per rupee is equal across goods, subject to the budget.</p>
<h3>3. Indifference curve and budget line</h3><p>An indifference curve shows combinations of two goods that give the consumer equal satisfaction. It normally slopes downward and is convex to the origin. A budget line shows combinations the consumer can buy with given income and prices.</p><div class="formula">Budget line: Px·X + Py·Y = M</div><p>M = money income; Px and Py are prices.</p>
<div class="diagram"><b>Consumer equilibrium (indifference curve and budget line)</b><svg viewBox="0 0 320 180" role="img" aria-label="Budget line tangent to an indifference curve at equilibrium point"><path d="M35 12V145H300" fill="none" stroke="#172334" stroke-width="1.5"/><path d="M45 25L265 135" stroke="#245a91" stroke-width="2.5"/><path d="M65 140 C85 65 165 35 250 20" stroke="#16845b" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none"/><circle cx="160" cy="82" r="4" fill="#b87916"/><text x="168" y="76" font-size="9" fill="#b87916">E</text><text x="205" y="140" font-size="9" fill="#245a91">Budget line</text><text x="190" y="35" font-size="9" fill="#16845b">Indifference curve</text><text x="8" y="17" font-size="9" fill="#172334">Good Y</text><text x="270" y="164" font-size="9" fill="#172334">Good X</text></svg><small>At an interior equilibrium, the budget line is tangent to the highest attainable indifference curve.</small></div>
<p><b>Equilibrium condition:</b> MRSxy = Px/Py, with the budget constraint satisfied. MRS is the amount of Y the consumer is willing to give up for one more unit of X while keeping satisfaction constant.</p>
<h3>Exam practice</h3><ol><li>Define TU and MU.</li><li>State the law of diminishing marginal utility.</li><li>Explain the equi-marginal utility principle.</li><li>Draw and explain a budget line and indifference curve.</li><li>State the consumer equilibrium condition using MRS.</li></ol>`;

window.COURSE_LESSONS.find(x=>x.n===15).content = `
<h3>Lesson 15 — Demand</h3><p><b>Public examination · Consumer Behaviour.</b></p>
<h3>1. Meaning and law of demand</h3><p>Demand is the quantity of a good that a consumer is willing and able to buy at different prices during a given period. <b>Law of demand:</b> other things remaining constant, quantity demanded generally rises when price falls and falls when price rises.</p>
<h3>2. Demand schedule and curve</h3><table class="study-table"><tr><th>Price (₹)</th><th>Quantity demanded</th></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>8</td></tr></table><p>This schedule shows an inverse relationship between price and quantity demanded.</p>
<div class="diagram"><b>Demand curve</b><svg viewBox="0 0 320 180" role="img" aria-label="Downward sloping demand curve"><path d="M35 12V145H300" fill="none" stroke="#172334" stroke-width="1.5"/><path d="M65 25L270 130" stroke="#245a91" stroke-width="3"/><text x="275" y="135" font-size="11" fill="#245a91">D</text><text x="8" y="18" font-size="9" fill="#172334">Price</text><text x="245" y="165" font-size="9" fill="#172334">Quantity</text></svg></div>
<h3>3. Determinants of demand</h3><ul><li>Consumer income.</li><li>Prices of related goods: substitutes and complements.</li><li>Tastes, preferences and fashion.</li><li>Number of consumers.</li><li>Expectations about future prices and income.</li><li>Season and other conditions.</li></ul>
<h3>4. Movement versus shift</h3><p><b>Change in quantity demanded:</b> caused by the good's own price; movement along the same demand curve. Price falls → extension; price rises → contraction.</p><p><b>Change in demand:</b> caused by a non-price factor; the entire curve shifts. Increase in demand → right shift; decrease → left shift.</p>
<div class="diagram"><b>Demand curve shift</b><svg viewBox="0 0 320 170" role="img" aria-label="Original demand curve shifts right from D1 to D2"><path d="M35 10V140H300" fill="none" stroke="#172334" stroke-width="1.5"/><path d="M65 25L230 125" stroke="#245a91" stroke-width="2.5"/><path d="M110 25L275 125" stroke="#16845b" stroke-width="2.5"/><text x="234" y="123" font-size="10" fill="#245a91">D1</text><text x="280" y="123" font-size="10" fill="#16845b">D2</text><text x="135" y="158" font-size="9" fill="#16845b">Right shift = increase in demand</text></svg></div>
<h3>Exam practice</h3><ol><li>State the law of demand and its assumptions.</li><li>Explain four determinants of demand.</li><li>Differentiate extension/contraction and increase/decrease in demand.</li><li>Explain how a change in income can shift demand.</li><li>Draw a demand curve and a rightward shift.</li></ol>
<div class="callout"><b>Memory rule:</b> Own price change = movement. Any other determinant change = shift.</div>`;

window.COURSE_LESSONS.find(x=>x.n===16).content = `
<h3>Lesson 16 — Price Elasticity of Demand</h3><p><b>Public examination · Consumer Behaviour.</b></p>
<h3>1. Meaning</h3><p>Price elasticity of demand measures how responsive quantity demanded is to a change in price.</p><div class="formula">Ed = (% change in quantity demanded) ÷ (% change in price)</div><p>Demand elasticity is usually negative because price and quantity demanded move in opposite directions. In many classifications, the absolute value is used.</p>
<h3>2. Percentage method</h3><div class="formula">Ed = (Change in Q ÷ Original Q) ÷ (Change in P ÷ Original P)</div><div class="worked"><b>Worked example</b><p>Price falls by 10%; quantity demanded rises by 20%.</p><div class="formula">|Ed| = 20 ÷ 10 = 2</div><p>Demand is elastic because the absolute value is greater than 1.</p></div>
<h3>3. Degrees of elasticity</h3><ul><li>Perfectly inelastic: Ed = 0.</li><li>Relatively inelastic: |Ed| is less than 1.</li><li>Unitary elastic: |Ed| = 1.</li><li>Relatively elastic: |Ed| is greater than 1.</li><li>Perfectly elastic: |Ed| is extremely large.</li></ul>
<h3>4. Total expenditure method</h3><p>Total expenditure (TE) = Price × Quantity.</p><table class="study-table"><tr><th>Price falls</th><th>Total expenditure</th><th>Inference</th></tr><tr><td>TE rises</td><td>Increase</td><td>Elastic demand</td></tr><tr><td>TE unchanged</td><td>No change</td><td>Unitary elastic</td></tr><tr><td>TE falls</td><td>Decrease</td><td>Inelastic demand</td></tr></table>
<h3>5. Factors affecting elasticity</h3><ul><li>Availability of close substitutes.</li><li>Whether the good is a necessity or luxury.</li><li>Share of income spent on the good.</li><li>Time available to adjust.</li><li>Number of uses of the good.</li></ul>
<h3>Exam practice</h3><ol><li>Define price elasticity of demand.</li><li>Calculate elasticity when price falls by 5% and quantity rises by 15%.</li><li>Explain the total expenditure method.</li><li>State five factors affecting elasticity.</li><li>Differentiate elastic, inelastic and unitary elastic demand.</li></ol>
<p class="note">Use absolute value when classifying elasticity. Write the percentage formula before substituting figures.</p>`;

window.COURSE_LESSONS.find(x=>x.n===21).content = `
<h3>Lesson 21 — Forms of Market</h3><p><b>Public examination · Market and Price Determination.</b></p>
<p>A market is an arrangement through which buyers and sellers interact to exchange goods and services. Market forms differ by number of firms, nature of product, entry barriers and control over price.</p>
<table class="study-table"><tr><th>Market form</th><th>Main features</th></tr><tr><td>Perfect competition</td><td>Many buyers and sellers; homogeneous product; free entry/exit; firms are price takers.</td></tr><tr><td>Monopoly</td><td>One seller; no close substitute; strong entry barriers; seller has significant price control.</td></tr><tr><td>Monopolistic competition</td><td>Many firms; differentiated products; some price control; selling costs/advertising important.</td></tr><tr><td>Oligopoly</td><td>Few large firms; interdependence; decisions of one firm affect others; entry barriers may exist.</td></tr></table>
<h3>Price taker versus price maker</h3><p>A perfectly competitive firm accepts the market price because one firm is too small to influence it. A monopolist faces the market demand curve and has price-setting power, but still cannot choose both any price and any quantity independently.</p>
<h3>Exam practice</h3><ol><li>Define market.</li><li>State four features of perfect competition.</li><li>Differentiate monopoly and monopolistic competition.</li><li>Why are firms in oligopoly interdependent?</li><li>Compare all four market forms by number of sellers and product type.</li></ol>`;

window.COURSE_LESSONS.find(x=>x.n===22).content = `
<h3>Lesson 22 — Price Determination Under Perfect Competition</h3><p><b>Public examination · Market and Price Determination.</b></p>
<h3>1. Equilibrium price</h3><p>Market equilibrium occurs where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied. The price at this point is the equilibrium price and the quantity is equilibrium quantity.</p>
<div class="diagram"><b>Market equilibrium</b><svg viewBox="0 0 320 180" role="img" aria-label="Demand and supply curves intersect at equilibrium E"><path d="M35 10V145H300" fill="none" stroke="#172334" stroke-width="1.5"/><path d="M60 25L270 130" stroke="#245a91" stroke-width="2.5"/><path d="M60 130L270 25" stroke="#16845b" stroke-width="2.5"/><circle cx="165" cy="77" r="4" fill="#b87916"/><path d="M165 77V145M35 77H165" stroke="#b87916" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="173" y="72" font-size="10" fill="#b87916">E</text><text x="274" y="132" font-size="10" fill="#245a91">D</text><text x="274" y="24" font-size="10" fill="#16845b">S</text><text x="7" y="17" font-size="9" fill="#172334">Price</text><text x="245" y="166" font-size="9" fill="#172334">Quantity</text></svg></div>
<h3>2. Excess demand and excess supply</h3><ul><li><b>Excess demand (shortage):</b> at a price below equilibrium, Qd is greater than Qs. Buyers compete, pushing price upward.</li><li><b>Excess supply (surplus):</b> at a price above equilibrium, Qs is greater than Qd. Sellers compete, pushing price downward.</li></ul>
<h3>3. Changes in demand and supply</h3><ul><li>Demand rises, supply unchanged → equilibrium price and quantity generally rise.</li><li>Demand falls, supply unchanged → equilibrium price and quantity generally fall.</li><li>Supply rises, demand unchanged → equilibrium price falls and quantity rises.</li><li>Supply falls, demand unchanged → equilibrium price rises and quantity falls.</li></ul>
<h3>4. Price controls</h3><p><b>Price ceiling:</b> a legal maximum price. If set below equilibrium, it can create shortage.</p><p><b>Price floor:</b> a legal minimum price. If set above equilibrium, it can create surplus.</p>
<h3>Exam practice</h3><ol><li>Define equilibrium price.</li><li>Explain how excess demand changes price.</li><li>Explain how excess supply changes price.</li><li>Show the effect of an increase in supply on a demand-supply diagram.</li><li>Differentiate price ceiling and price floor.</li></ol>`;

window.COURSE_LESSONS.find(x=>x.n===23).content = `
<h3>Lesson 23 — Revenue and Profit Maximization of a Competitive Firm</h3><p><b>Public examination · Market and Price Determination.</b></p>
<h3>1. Revenue concepts</h3><ul><li><b>Total Revenue (TR):</b> price multiplied by quantity sold.</li><li><b>Average Revenue (AR):</b> revenue per unit sold.</li><li><b>Marginal Revenue (MR):</b> additional revenue from selling one more unit.</li></ul><div class="formula">TR = P × Q<br>AR = TR ÷ Q<br>MR = Change in TR ÷ Change in Q</div><p>Under perfect competition, a firm sells at the market price, so P = AR = MR.</p>
<h3>2. Profit</h3><div class="formula">Profit = Total Revenue − Total Cost</div><p>A firm chooses output to maximise the difference between TR and TC.</p>
<h3>3. Profit-maximising conditions</h3><ul><li><b>Total approach:</b> choose the output where the positive difference between TR and TC is greatest.</li><li><b>Marginal approach:</b> produce where MR = MC, and MC should be rising/cut MR from below for a maximum.</li></ul><p>If MR is greater than MC, an extra unit adds more revenue than cost, so increasing output raises profit. If MC is greater than MR, reducing output can raise profit.</p>
<div class="diagram"><b>Profit maximisation — MR and MC</b><svg viewBox="0 0 320 170" role="img" aria-label="Horizontal MR line intersects rising MC at profit-maximising output"><path d="M35 10V140H300" fill="none" stroke="#172334" stroke-width="1.5"/><path d="M45 75H280" stroke="#245a91" stroke-width="2.5"/><path d="M55 130 C115 125 155 100 190 70 S245 25 280 15" stroke="#16845b" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none"/><path d="M175 75V140" stroke="#b87916" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="282" y="73" font-size="9" fill="#245a91">MR=AR=P</text><text x="282" y="15" font-size="9" fill="#16845b">MC</text><text x="180" y="154" font-size="9" fill="#b87916">Q*</text><text x="8" y="17" font-size="9" fill="#172334">Cost/Revenue</text><text x="265" y="166" font-size="9" fill="#172334">Output</text></svg></div>
<h3>Exam practice</h3><ol><li>Define TR, AR and MR.</li><li>Calculate TR when price is ₹12 and quantity is 8 units.</li><li>Why is AR = MR = price for a competitive firm?</li><li>Explain profit maximisation using TR-TC and MR-MC approaches.</li></ol>`;

window.COURSE_LESSONS.find(x=>x.n===24).content = `
<h3>Lesson 24 — National Income and Related Aggregates</h3><p><b>Public examination · National Income Accounting.</b></p>
<h3>1. Basic ideas</h3><ul><li><b>Final good:</b> purchased for final use, not for resale or further production in the same period.</li><li><b>Intermediate good:</b> used as an input or for resale; its value is included in the final good to avoid double counting.</li><li><b>Factor income:</b> income earned for providing land, labour, capital or enterprise.</li><li><b>Stock:</b> measured at a point in time (e.g. wealth). <b>Flow:</b> measured over a period (e.g. income).</li></ul>
<h3>2. Domestic and national</h3><p><b>Domestic product</b> relates to production within the domestic territory. <b>National product</b> relates to production/income of normal residents. The difference is Net Factor Income from Abroad (NFIA).</p><div class="formula">GNP = GDP + NFIA</div>
<h3>3. GDP, NDP, GNP and NNP</h3><div class="formula">NDP = GDP − Depreciation<br>GNP = GDP + NFIA<br>NNP = GNP − Depreciation<br>Net Indirect Taxes = Indirect Taxes − Subsidies<br>Factor Cost = Market Price − Net Indirect Taxes</div><p>Use matching price bases (market price or factor cost) consistently. National Income is commonly defined as NNP at factor cost in the traditional syllabus terminology.</p>
<h3>4. Circular flow of income</h3><p>In a simple two-sector economy, households provide factor services to firms and receive factor income. Firms produce goods and services; households spend income to buy them. Real flows and money flows move in opposite directions.</p>
<div class="diagram"><b>Two-sector circular flow</b><svg viewBox="0 0 330 165" role="img" aria-label="Households provide factor services to firms and receive income; firms provide goods and receive consumption spending"><rect x="8" y="50" width="115" height="52" rx="8" fill="#eaf2fb" stroke="#245a91"/><text x="65" y="80" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="#172334">Households</text><rect x="207" y="50" width="115" height="52" rx="8" fill="#eaf7f0" stroke="#16845b"/><text x="264" y="80" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="#172334">Firms</text><path d="M123 60H200M207 92H130" stroke="#245a91" stroke-width="2"/><text x="165" y="48" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#245a91">Factor services</text><text x="165" y="112" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#16845b">Factor income</text><path d="M264 102V145H65V102" fill="none" stroke="#16845b" stroke-width="2"/><text x="165" y="158" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#16845b">Goods/services ↔ consumption spending</text></svg></div>
<h3>Exam practice</h3><ol><li>Differentiate final and intermediate goods.</li><li>Differentiate stock and flow.</li><li>Define GDP and GNP. Write the relation between them.</li><li>Explain depreciation and NFIA.</li><li>Draw the circular flow of income in a two-sector economy.</li></ol>`;

window.COURSE_LESSONS.find(x=>x.n===25).content = `
<h3>Lesson 25 — National Income and its Measurement</h3><p><b>Public examination · National Income Accounting.</b></p>
<h3>1. Three methods</h3><ul><li><b>Product/value-added method:</b> add value added by all producing units.</li><li><b>Income method:</b> add factor incomes earned in production.</li><li><b>Expenditure method:</b> add final expenditure on goods and services.</li></ul>
<h3>2. Formulas</h3><div class="formula">Value Added = Value of Output − Intermediate Consumption<br>GDP (Expenditure) = C + I + G + (X − M)<br>National Income (traditional) = NNP at Factor Cost</div><p>C = private final consumption; I = investment; G = government final consumption; X = exports; M = imports.</p>
<h3>3. Precautions</h3><ul><li>Do not count intermediate goods separately when final goods are counted.</li><li>Exclude transfer payments because they are not payment for current production.</li><li>Exclude purchase of second-hand goods, but include current brokerage/commission on their sale.</li><li>Include production for own final consumption when required by the method and data.</li><li>Include only factor incomes earned from current production in the income method.</li></ul>
<div class="worked"><b>Worked value-added example</b><p>A farmer sells wheat for ₹100 to a mill. The mill sells flour for ₹160 to a baker. The baker sells bread for ₹250 to consumers.</p><ul><li>Farmer value added = ₹100</li><li>Mill value added = ₹160 − ₹100 = ₹60</li><li>Baker value added = ₹250 − ₹160 = ₹90</li></ul><div class="formula">Total value added = 100 + 60 + 90 = ₹250</div><p>This equals the final bread value. Adding all sales (100+160+250) would double count inputs.</p></div>
<h3>4. Convert market price to factor cost</h3><div class="formula">Factor Cost = Market Price − Indirect Taxes + Subsidies</div><p>Remember: subtract net indirect taxes (indirect taxes minus subsidies) from market price.</p>
<h3>Exam practice</h3><ol><li>Explain the product, income and expenditure methods.</li><li>Why are intermediate goods excluded from final expenditure totals?</li><li>Calculate value added at each stage from a production chain.</li><li>Write the expenditure method formula.</li><li>Why are transfer payments excluded from national income?</li></ol>`;

window.COURSE_LESSONS.find(x=>x.n===26).content = `
<h3>Lesson 26 — Consumption, Saving and Investment</h3><p><b>Public examination · Theory of Income and Employment.</b></p>
<h3>1. Consumption and saving</h3><p>Consumption (C) is spending on goods and services. Saving (S) is the part of disposable income not consumed.</p><div class="formula">Y = C + S<br>S = Y − C</div>
<h3>2. Consumption function</h3><p>The consumption function shows the relationship between consumption and income. A simple form is:</p><div class="formula">C = a + bY</div><p>a = autonomous consumption; b = marginal propensity to consume (MPC); Y = income.</p>
<h3>3. Average and marginal propensities</h3><div class="formula">APC = C ÷ Y<br>APS = S ÷ Y<br>MPC = Change in C ÷ Change in Y<br>MPS = Change in S ÷ Change in Y<br>APC + APS = 1<br>MPC + MPS = 1</div>
<div class="worked"><b>Example</b><p>Income rises from ₹1,000 to ₹1,200. Consumption rises from ₹800 to ₹940.</p><div class="formula">MPC = (940−800) ÷ (1200−1000) = 140÷200 = 0.7</div><p>If the extra saving is ₹60, MPS = 60÷200 = 0.3. MPC + MPS = 1.</p></div>
<h3>4. Investment</h3><p>Investment is expenditure on adding to the economy's stock of capital goods, such as machinery, buildings and inventories. In the simple Keynesian model, planned investment may be treated as autonomous for the basic income-determination exercise.</p>
<h3>Exam practice</h3><ol><li>Define consumption and saving.</li><li>Write the consumption function.</li><li>Calculate MPC and MPS from changes in income, consumption and saving.</li><li>Show that MPC + MPS = 1.</li><li>What is investment in macroeconomics?</li></ol>`;

window.COURSE_LESSONS.find(x=>x.n===27).content = `
<h3>Lesson 27 — Theory of Income Determination</h3><p><b>Public examination · Theory of Income and Employment.</b></p>
<h3>1. Aggregate demand and equilibrium</h3><p>In a simple two-sector model, aggregate demand (AD) is planned consumption plus planned investment.</p><div class="formula">AD = C + I</div><p>Equilibrium level of income occurs where planned aggregate demand equals output/income (AS), so AD = Y. If planned spending exceeds output, inventories fall and firms increase production; if planned spending is below output, inventories rise and firms reduce production.</p>
<h3>2. Investment multiplier</h3><p>The multiplier shows how an initial change in autonomous investment can create a larger total change in equilibrium income.</p><div class="formula">k = Change in income ÷ Change in investment<br>k = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) = 1 ÷ MPS</div><div class="worked"><b>Example</b><p>If MPC = 0.8, then MPS = 0.2 and multiplier k = 1/0.2 = 5. If investment rises by ₹100 crore, the model predicts income rises by ₹500 crore, assuming the model's conditions hold.</p></div>
<h3>3. Excess and deficient demand</h3><ul><li><b>Excess demand:</b> AD is greater than the level needed for full-employment output; creates an inflationary gap and upward pressure on prices.</li><li><b>Deficient demand:</b> AD is insufficient for full-employment output; creates a deflationary gap, idle capacity and unemployment.</li></ul>
<h3>4. Corrective policy (basic idea)</h3><p>To reduce excess demand, policy may reduce spending or tighten monetary conditions. To address deficient demand, policy may increase public spending, reduce taxes or ease monetary conditions. Exact measures depend on the economic situation.</p>
<div class="diagram"><b>Keynesian cross — equilibrium</b><svg viewBox="0 0 320 180" role="img" aria-label="Aggregate demand curve crosses the 45 degree line at equilibrium income"><path d="M35 10V145H300" fill="none" stroke="#172334" stroke-width="1.5"/><path d="M40 140L275 18" stroke="#627187" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><path d="M40 112L275 30" stroke="#245a91" stroke-width="3"/><circle cx="160" cy="78" r="4" fill="#16845b"/><path d="M160 78V145" stroke="#16845b" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="170" y="72" font-size="9" fill="#16845b">E</text><text x="8" y="17" font-size="9" fill="#172334">AD</text><text x="270" y="165" font-size="9" fill="#172334">Income</text><text x="215" y="18" font-size="8" fill="#627187">45° line</text></svg></div>
<h3>Exam practice</h3><ol><li>Define aggregate demand.</li><li>State the equilibrium condition.</li><li>Calculate multiplier when MPC = 0.75.</li><li>Explain the multiplier process.</li><li>Differentiate excess demand and deficient demand.</li></ol>`;

window.COURSE_LESSONS.find(x=>x.n===28).content = `
<h3>Lesson 28 — Money and Banking</h3><p><b>Public examination · Money, Banking and Government Budget.</b></p>
<h3>1. Barter and money</h3><p>Barter is direct exchange of goods for goods. It requires a double coincidence of wants: each person must want what the other offers. Money removes this difficulty.</p>
<h3>2. Functions of money</h3><ul><li>Medium of exchange.</li><li>Measure/unit of value.</li><li>Store of value.</li><li>Standard of deferred payments.</li></ul>
<h3>3. Commercial banks and credit creation</h3><p>Commercial banks accept deposits, provide loans, facilitate payments and provide other financial services. By lending a portion of deposits while keeping required reserves, banks can create deposit money. Actual credit creation is limited by reserve requirements, cash withdrawals, demand for loans and bank behaviour.</p><p>In a highly simplified model, money multiplier = 1 ÷ reserve ratio. This is a theoretical simplification, not a complete description of real-world money creation.</p>
<h3>4. Central bank</h3><p>The central bank issues currency, acts as banker to the government and banks, holds reserves, manages payment/settlement systems, and uses monetary policy to influence liquidity, credit and inflation.</p>
<h3>5. Monetary policy tools</h3><ul><li>Policy interest rates/bank rate and repo-related tools.</li><li>Cash reserve ratio (CRR) and statutory liquidity requirements.</li><li>Open market operations (buying/selling government securities).</li></ul><p>Broadly, tightening tools can reduce liquidity/credit growth; easing tools can increase it. Do not memorise today's rate as a permanent fact—rates change.</p>
<h3>Exam practice</h3><ol><li>Explain the double coincidence of wants problem.</li><li>State four functions of money.</li><li>Explain how commercial banks create credit.</li><li>State four functions of the central bank.</li><li>Explain how open market operations can influence money supply.</li></ol>`;

window.COURSE_LESSONS.find(x=>x.n===29).content = `
<h3>Lesson 29 — Government and the Budget</h3><p><b>Public examination · Money, Banking and Government Budget.</b></p>
<h3>1. Meaning and objectives</h3><p>A government budget is an annual statement of the government's estimated receipts and expenditure for a financial year.</p><ul><li>Reallocation of resources toward public priorities.</li><li>Reduction of income and wealth inequalities.</li><li>Economic stability and control of inflation/deflation.</li><li>Economic growth and provision of public goods/services.</li></ul>
<h3>2. Receipts</h3><table class="study-table"><tr><th>Revenue receipts</th><th>Capital receipts</th></tr><tr><td>Do not create a liability and do not reduce assets. Examples: taxes, fees, interest receipts.</td><td>Create a liability or reduce assets. Examples: borrowings, recovery of loans, disinvestment proceeds.</td></tr></table>
<h3>3. Expenditure</h3><table class="study-table"><tr><th>Revenue expenditure</th><th>Capital expenditure</th></tr><tr><td>Generally does not create assets or reduce liabilities. Examples: salaries, pensions, subsidies, interest payments.</td><td>Creates assets or reduces liabilities. Examples: roads, schools, machinery, repayment of loans.</td></tr></table>
<h3>4. Budget deficits</h3><div class="formula">Revenue Deficit = Revenue Expenditure − Revenue Receipts<br>Fiscal Deficit = Total Expenditure − (Revenue Receipts + Non-debt Capital Receipts)<br>Primary Deficit = Fiscal Deficit − Interest Payments</div><p>Revenue deficit shows the shortfall in the revenue account. Fiscal deficit indicates the government's total borrowing requirement (under the stated definition). Primary deficit removes interest payments from fiscal deficit.</p>
<div class="worked"><b>Worked example</b><p>Revenue expenditure ₹8 lakh crore; revenue receipts ₹6 lakh crore; fiscal deficit ₹3 lakh crore; interest payments ₹1 lakh crore.</p><ul><li>Revenue deficit = 8 − 6 = ₹2 lakh crore.</li><li>Primary deficit = 3 − 1 = ₹2 lakh crore.</li></ul></div>
<h3>5. Fiscal policy</h3><p>Fiscal policy uses government taxation, spending and borrowing to influence economic activity, employment, distribution and price stability. Expansionary fiscal policy generally raises spending or reduces taxes; contractionary policy generally reduces spending or raises taxes.</p>
<h3>Exam practice</h3><ol><li>Define government budget and state its objectives.</li><li>Differentiate revenue and capital receipts.</li><li>Differentiate revenue and capital expenditure.</li><li>Calculate revenue deficit, fiscal deficit and primary deficit.</li><li>Explain fiscal policy.</li></ol>
<p class="note">In deficit numericals, write the formula first and use only the exact receipt/expenditure category named in that formula.</p>`;
